1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,956
Human activities are destroying natural habitats. Forests are cut down for farms, logging and homes.

2
00:00:05,956 --> 00:00:10,674
This is deforestation, and it's destroying the habitats of thousands of species.

3
00:00:11,733 --> 00:00:18,220
Taking away one species affects a whole ecosystem. Bats eat the fruit of banyan trees and spread their seeds.

4
00:00:18,220 --> 00:00:23,705
Without banyan trees, bat numbers would fall, and so would the owls and hawks that eat bats.

5
00:00:23,705 --> 00:00:27,951
Forests are also carbon sinks, absorbing more carbon than they release,

6
00:00:27,951 --> 00:00:30,310
so deforestation adds to climate change.

7
00:00:31,369 --> 00:00:35,674
Roads, cities and farms can also break large habitats into small pieces.

8
00:00:35,674 --> 00:00:40,864
This habitat fragmentation makes it harder for animals to find food, water and shelter,

9
00:00:40,864 --> 00:00:42,338
and reduces biodiversity.

10
00:00:43,397 --> 00:00:49,707
Overexploitation means removing too many animals from the wild, for example by overfishing or overhunting.

11
00:00:49,707 --> 00:00:56,077
When fish are caught faster than they can reproduce, populations fall, and other organisms lose their food.

12
00:00:56,077 --> 00:01:00,146
Some modern fishing methods scoop up everything from the ocean floor.

13
00:01:01,205 --> 00:01:04,920
Sometimes humans bring a non-native species into an ecosystem.

14
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:10,051
The Nile perch was put into Lake Victoria in East Africa as food, but it's a predator,

15
00:01:10,051 --> 00:01:15,005
and it wiped out many native fish species, harming birds and mammals that ate them.

16
00:01:15,005 --> 00:01:18,956
A non-native species that causes this much harm is called invasive.

17
00:01:20,015 --> 00:01:25,618
Some species arrive by accident, with cargo and travellers. Asian hornets prey on native bees,

18
00:01:25,618 --> 00:01:31,043
which are vital pollinators, and rats on ships have driven many native birds to extinction.

19
00:01:31,043 --> 00:01:36,528
On the positive side, re-introducing lost species, like wolves in Yellowstone National Park,

20
00:01:36,528 --> 00:01:38,179
can help ecosystems recover.

21
00:01:39,238 --> 00:01:45,725
Pollution harms ecosystems. Land pollution comes from litter, plastics and chemicals that seep into the soil.

22
00:01:45,725 --> 00:01:49,794
Rotting rubbish in landfills even releases methane, a greenhouse gas.

23
00:01:50,853 --> 00:01:54,569
Air pollution comes from factory smoke, car exhausts and dust.

24
00:01:54,569 --> 00:02:00,761
Sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides dissolve in clouds and make acid rain, which harms plants and animals.

25
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:07,364
Water pollution comes from fertilisers, pesticides, factory chemicals, untreated waste water,

26
00:02:07,364 --> 00:02:08,720
oil spills and litter.

27
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:14,558
Fertilisers make algae grow out of control, an algal bloom, blocking sunlight so water plants die.

28
00:02:14,558 --> 00:02:18,215
Oil sticks to feathers and fur, turtles swallow plastic bags,

29
00:02:18,215 --> 00:02:21,281
and tiny microplastics have entered our food chains.

30
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:27,825
Two jars contain the same pond water. A few drops of garden fertiliser are added to one jar.

31
00:02:27,825 --> 00:02:32,838
Both are left in sunlight for two weeks. Predict: what will happen? Pause and decide.

32
00:02:33,897 --> 00:02:37,494
Watch. Both jars stand in the same sunny place for two weeks.

33
00:02:38,553 --> 00:02:44,215
The jar with fertiliser has turned bright green with algae. The other jar is still almost clear.

34
00:02:45,274 --> 00:02:50,287
Explain what you saw. Fertiliser contains nutrients that help plants and algae grow.

35
00:02:50,287 --> 00:02:56,361
When fertiliser washes off farmland into rivers and lakes, algae grow very fast and cover the surface.

36
00:02:56,361 --> 00:03:02,140
They block light, and when they die, bacteria that decompose them use up the oxygen in the water,

37
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:05,207
so fish die. Pause and explain it in your own words.

38
00:03:06,266 --> 00:03:11,633
This chart shows typical amounts of mercury in organisms along a polluted lake food chain,

39
00:03:11,633 --> 00:03:12,871
in parts per million.

40
00:03:13,930 --> 00:03:16,112
Plankton contain only a tiny amount.

41
00:03:16,112 --> 00:03:21,656
Small fish have more, large fish more again, and the fish-eating bird at the top has the most.

42
00:03:22,715 --> 00:03:27,904
Pause and decide: why does the top predator have so much more mercury than the plankton?

43
00:03:28,963 --> 00:03:34,920
Some toxic substances, like mercury from mining and manufacturing, can't be broken down or excreted.

44
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:40,345
When they get into water, tiny plankton absorb small amounts, and it stays in their bodies.

45
00:03:40,345 --> 00:03:42,999
This slow build-up is called bioaccumulation.

46
00:03:44,058 --> 00:03:47,773
Small fish eat lots of plankton, so they take in more mercury.

47
00:03:47,773 --> 00:03:51,076
Larger fish eat many small fish, so they get even more.

48
00:03:51,076 --> 00:03:54,379
The amount gets higher and higher along the food chain.

49
00:03:54,379 --> 00:03:59,627
This is biomagnification, and people who eat contaminated fish can get mercury poisoning.

50
00:04:00,686 --> 00:04:06,171
Now it is your turn to shine! Pause the video after each question and try to answer it first.

51
00:04:07,230 --> 00:04:12,714
Question 1: What is the difference between a population and a community? Pause the video now.

52
00:04:13,773 --> 00:04:18,668
Answer: A population is one species; a community is all the populations in an area.

53
00:04:19,727 --> 00:04:24,150
Question 2: Give one physical adaptation of a dolphin. Pause the video now.

54
00:04:25,209 --> 00:04:29,219
Answer: for example streamlined body, blowhole, blubber or flippers.

55
00:04:30,278 --> 00:04:33,876
Question 3: What is an invasive species? Pause the video now.

56
00:04:34,935 --> 00:04:38,886
Answer: A non-native species that causes harm to its new ecosystem.

57
00:04:39,945 --> 00:04:45,371
Question 4: Why do top predators have the most mercury in their bodies? Pause the video now.

58
00:04:46,430 --> 00:04:50,263
Answer: Biomagnification, mercury builds up along the food chain.
